\section{Methodology Overview}

This section outlines the comprehensive methodology employed in conducting the ESG analysis for Indonesian companies, with particular emphasis on the unique characteristics of the Indonesian market and regulatory environment.

\subsection{Data Collection Framework}

The data collection process follows a multi-source approach, combining quantitative metrics from financial statements with qualitative assessments from corporate disclosures. Primary data sources include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Annual reports and sustainability reports published by companies
    \item Bapepam-LK (now OJK) disclosure requirements
    \item IDX ESG disclosure requirements
    \item Third-party ESG ratings and assessments
\end{itemize}

The methodology incorporates both mandatory and voluntary disclosures, recognizing that Indonesian companies vary significantly in their reporting maturity. Data collection spans a minimum of three years to establish trend analysis capabilities.

\subsection{ESG Factor Weighting System}

The ESG factors are weighted according to their materiality in the Indonesian context, with specific adjustments for sector-specific considerations:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\textbf{ESG Category} & \textbf{Weight} & \textbf{Indonesian Context Considerations} \\
\hline
Environmental & 35\% & Climate vulnerability, deforestation risks \\
Social & 35\% & Labor rights, community relations, diversity \\
Governance & 30\% & Board independence, corruption risks, regulatory compliance \\
\end{tabular}
\caption{ESG Factor Weighting System}
\end{table}

\subsection{Analytical Framework}

The analytical framework employs a hybrid approach combining quantitative scoring with qualitative assessment:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quantitative Analysis}: Financial and operational metrics are normalized and scored using z-score methodology
    \item \textbf{Qualitative Assessment}: Management practices, policies, and strategic initiatives are evaluated using a structured rubric
    \item \textbf{Contextual Adjustment}: Scores are adjusted based on Indonesia-specific factors such as regulatory environment and market conditions
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Risk Assessment Methodology}

ESG risks are assessed using a two-dimensional matrix considering both likelihood and impact:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Environmental Risks}: Climate change exposure, resource scarcity, pollution
    \item \textbf{Social Risks}: Labor practices, community relations, supply chain management
    \item \textbf{Governance Risks}: Corporate ethics, board structure, regulatory compliance
\end{itemize}

Each risk factor is scored on a scale of 1-5 for both likelihood and impact, with the final risk score calculated as the product of these two dimensions.

\subsection{Performance Benchmarking}

Company performance is benchmarked against:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Industry peers within Indonesia
    \item Regional ASEAN standards
    \item Global best practices
\end{itemize}

The benchmarking process includes both absolute and relative performance measures, with particular attention to Indonesia-specific challenges and opportunities.

\subsection{Validation Process}

The methodology includes multiple validation steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Cross-referencing of data from multiple sources
    \item Peer review by ESG experts familiar with Indonesian markets
    \item Back-testing of methodology using historical data
    \item Sensitivity analysis to test robustness of findings
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Reporting Framework}

Results are presented using a standardized reporting framework that includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Executive summary with key findings
    \item Detailed methodology description
    \item Sector-specific analysis
    \item Company-level assessments
    \item Recommendations for improvement
\end{itemize}

The framework is designed to be transparent and reproducible, allowing stakeholders to understand the basis for conclusions and recommendations.

\subsection{Limitations and Considerations}

The methodology acknowledges several limitations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Data quality and availability variations across companies
    \item Subjectivity in qualitative assessments
    \item Rapidly evolving ESG landscape in Indonesia
    \item Potential for greenwashing in corporate disclosures
\end{itemize}

These limitations are addressed through conservative assumptions and multiple validation steps.

\subsection{Continuous Improvement Process}

The methodology includes a built-in continuous improvement process:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Regular review of emerging ESG issues
    \item Updates to weighting schemes based on market evolution
    \item Incorporation of stakeholder feedback
    \item Refinement of assessment criteria
\end{enumerate}

This ensures the methodology remains relevant and effective in the dynamic Indonesian ESG landscape.

