\section{Risk Management and Compliance}

\subsection{ESG Risk Assessment Framework}

The Indonesian financial sector has developed a comprehensive ESG risk assessment framework that aligns with international best practices while addressing local specificities. According to the Financial Services Authority (OJK) guidelines, financial institutions must evaluate ESG risks across three dimensions: physical risks (climate-related disasters, resource scarcity), transition risks (policy changes, technological shifts), and liability risks (legal claims, reputational damage) \cite{OJK_2020}.

The framework requires banks to implement a scoring system that quantifies ESG risks on a scale of 1-5, with specific weightings for different sectors. For instance, mining and energy companies receive higher risk scores due to their environmental impact, while technology firms are primarily assessed on governance factors. The assessment must consider both direct and indirect exposures, including supply chain risks and counterparty relationships.

\subsection{Regulatory Compliance Requirements}

Indonesian financial institutions must comply with multiple layers of ESG-related regulations. The OJK's Sustainable Finance Roadmap (2021-2025) mandates that all banks conduct ESG due diligence for transactions exceeding IDR 100 billion \cite{OJK_Sustainable_Finance}. This includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Environmental risk assessment for projects in sensitive areas
    \item Social impact analysis for community displacement
    \item Governance evaluation of corporate structures
\end{itemize}

Banks must also adhere to the Indonesian Sustainable Finance Taxonomy, which categorizes economic activities based on their environmental impact. The taxonomy uses a traffic light system: green for sustainable activities, yellow for transition activities, and red for activities that must be phased out.

\subsection{Climate Risk Management}

Climate risk has emerged as a critical component of ESG risk management in Indonesia. Financial institutions must assess both physical risks (e.g., flooding, sea-level rise) and transition risks (e.g., carbon pricing, renewable energy policies). The Indonesian Financial System Stability Committee (KSSK) requires banks to conduct climate scenario analysis and stress testing \cite{KSSK_2022}.

Key requirements include:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Integration of climate risks into credit risk assessment
    \item Disclosure of climate-related financial risks
    \item Development of green financing products
\end{enumerate}

Banks must also align with the Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) recommendations, adapting them to the Indonesian context. This includes scenario analysis for a 2°C warming pathway and assessment of transition risks associated with Indonesia's commitment to achieve net-zero emissions by 2060.

\subsection{Social Risk Management}

Social risk management in Indonesia focuses on community relations, labor practices, and human rights. Financial institutions must evaluate:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Indigenous peoples' rights and land ownership issues
    \item Labor standards and working conditions
    \item Community health and safety impacts
    \item Cultural heritage preservation
\end{itemize}

The Indonesian National Committee on Human Rights (Komnas HAM) has developed specific guidelines for financial institutions to assess social risks in their portfolios. Banks must conduct human rights due diligence, particularly for projects in sensitive areas such as Papua and West Papua provinces.

\subsection{Governance Risk Framework}

Governance risk assessment focuses on corporate structures, anti-corruption measures, and transparency. Indonesian banks must evaluate:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Board composition and independence
    \item Anti-money laundering (AML) controls
    \item Conflict of interest policies
    \item Whistleblower protection mechanisms
\end{enumerate}

The OJK requires banks to implement a three-line defense model for ESG risk management:

\begin{itemize}
    \item First line: Business units responsible for risk management
    \item Second line: Risk management and compliance functions
    \item Third line: Internal audit
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compliance Monitoring and Reporting}

Financial institutions must establish robust monitoring and reporting systems for ESG compliance. Key requirements include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Quarterly ESG risk reports to the board of directors
    \item Annual ESG disclosure statements
    \item Regular internal audits of ESG compliance
    \item External verification of ESG disclosures
\end{itemize}

The OJK has developed specific reporting templates that banks must use to disclose their ESG risk management practices. These templates include:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item ESG risk assessment methodology
    \item Key risk indicators (KRIs)
    \item Mitigation strategies
    \item Performance metrics
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Remediation and Escalation Procedures}

Banks must establish clear procedures for addressing ESG compliance breaches. The framework requires:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Immediate notification to senior management for material breaches
    \item Development of remediation plans within 30 days
    \item Regular progress monitoring
    \item Escalation to the board for unresolved issues
\end{itemize}

The OJK has the authority to impose sanctions for non-compliance, ranging from administrative warnings to license revocation. Banks must maintain a breach register and report all material breaches to the regulator within 15 days.

\subsection{Integration with Enterprise Risk Management}

ESG risk management must be fully integrated with the bank's overall enterprise risk management (ERM) framework. This includes:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Incorporation of ESG risks into the bank's risk appetite statement
    \item Integration of ESG metrics into performance management systems
    \item Alignment of ESG risk limits with overall risk limits
    \item Regular reporting to the ERM committee
\end{enumerate}

Banks must also ensure that their ESG risk management framework is consistent with international standards such as the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision (BCBS) principles for effective risk data aggregation and risk reporting.

\subsection{Technology and Data Management}

Effective ESG risk management requires robust technology and data management systems. Banks must:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Implement ESG-specific risk assessment tools
    \item Develop data collection and validation processes
    \item Ensure data quality and consistency
    \item Maintain audit trails for ESG assessments
\end{itemize}

The OJK has established a central database for ESG-related information, which banks must contribute to and draw from. This includes data on:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Environmental impact assessments
    \item Social impact studies
    \item Governance ratings
    \item Climate risk scenarios
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Capacity Building and Training}

Banks must invest in capacity building and training to ensure effective ESG risk management. This includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Regular training programs for staff on ESG risks
    \item Certification programs for ESG specialists
    \item Knowledge sharing platforms
    \item External expert consultations
\end{itemize}

The Indonesian Banking Institute (IBI) has developed specific training modules on ESG risk management, which banks must ensure their staff complete. These modules cover:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item ESG risk assessment methodologies
    \item Regulatory requirements
    \item Case studies and best practices
    \item Emerging trends and challenges
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Continuous Improvement and Review}

ESG risk management frameworks must be subject to regular review and improvement. Banks must:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Conduct annual reviews of their ESG risk management framework
    \item Update policies and procedures based on emerging risks
    \item Benchmark against industry best practices
    \item Seek feedback from stakeholders
\end{itemize}

The OJK conducts regular reviews of banks' ESG risk management practices and may require enhancements based on:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Changes in regulatory requirements
    \item Emerging ESG risks
    \item Industry developments
    \item Stakeholder feedback
\end{enumerate}

This comprehensive approach to ESG risk management and compliance ensures that Indonesian financial institutions can effectively identify, assess, and mitigate ESG risks while meeting regulatory requirements and contributing to sustainable development.

