\section{Global Best Practices}

\subsection{International ESG Frameworks}

Indonesia's ESG landscape is increasingly aligned with global best practices, particularly through the adoption of internationally recognized frameworks. The Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) has gained significant traction, with the Indonesian Financial Services Authority (OJK) mandating TCFD-aligned disclosures for financial institutions starting in 2023 \cite{ojk_tcfd}. This alignment demonstrates Indonesia's commitment to standardized climate risk reporting and transparency.

The Sustainability Accounting Standards Board (SASB) standards have also been incorporated into Indonesian corporate reporting practices, particularly for sectors with significant environmental impacts such as mining, palm oil, and energy. Companies like PT Astra International and Bank Central Asia have begun integrating SASB metrics into their sustainability reports, providing industry-specific ESG performance indicators that facilitate comparability with global peers \cite{sasb_indonesia}.

\subsection{Paris Agreement Alignment}

As a signatory to the Paris Agreement, Indonesia has committed to reducing greenhouse gas emissions by 29\% (or 41\% with international support) by 2030 compared to business-as-usual scenarios \cite{ndc_indonesia}. This commitment has driven the development of national policies that align with global climate action, including the National Action Plan for Greenhouse Gas Reduction and the Long-Term Strategy for Low Carbon and Climate Resilience 2050.

The financial sector has responded with initiatives such as the Indonesia Sustainable Finance Roadmap, which aims to mobilize private capital for sustainable development projects. The Indonesia Green Investment Platform, launched in 2022, connects international investors with domestic sustainable projects, demonstrating alignment with global sustainable finance trends \cite{green_investment_platform}.

\subsection{UN Sustainable Development Goals Integration}

Indonesian companies are increasingly aligning their ESG strategies with the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). The government's National Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMN) 2020-2024 explicitly incorporates SDG targets, creating a framework for corporate alignment \cite{rpjmn_2020}. Major corporations including Unilever Indonesia and Indofood have mapped their sustainability initiatives to specific SDG targets, particularly in areas such as responsible consumption, climate action, and sustainable communities.

The Indonesia Business Council for Sustainable Development (IBCSD) serves as a platform for companies to share best practices and collaborate on SDG implementation. Through initiatives like the SDG Indonesia One platform, businesses are connecting with development projects that address multiple SDGs simultaneously, demonstrating a holistic approach to sustainable development \cite{ibcsd_sdgs}.

\subsection{Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) Standards}

The Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) Standards have become the dominant framework for sustainability reporting in Indonesia. According to the Indonesian Sustainability Reporting Awards (ISRA), over 80\% of participating companies use GRI standards in their sustainability reports \cite{isra_2022}. This widespread adoption facilitates comparability and benchmarking against international peers.

The Indonesian Institute of Accountants (IAI) has integrated GRI standards into the Indonesian Sustainability Reporting Guide, creating a localized framework that maintains global compatibility. This integration has been particularly important for state-owned enterprises, which are required to publish sustainability reports aligned with both GRI and national guidelines \cite{iai_sustainability}.

\subsection{Emerging Best Practices in ESG Integration}

Recent developments in Indonesian ESG practices reflect global trends toward integrated reporting and double materiality assessment. The ASEAN Sustainability Standards (ASS), launched in 2023, provide a regional framework that aligns with both global standards and local contexts \cite{asean_sustainability}. This framework addresses the unique challenges faced by Southeast Asian companies while maintaining compatibility with international reporting requirements.

Corporate governance practices are also evolving to incorporate ESG considerations. The Indonesian Corporate Governance Guidelines now include recommendations for board-level oversight of sustainability issues, following global best practices in risk management and strategic planning \cite{oic_gcg}. Companies are establishing dedicated sustainability committees and linking executive compensation to ESG performance metrics, practices that have become standard in leading global corporations.

Stakeholder engagement practices are becoming more sophisticated, with companies conducting regular materiality assessments and engaging with diverse stakeholder groups. The use of digital platforms for ESG disclosure and stakeholder communication is increasing, following global trends toward transparency and accessibility. These practices align with the principles of dynamic materiality, recognizing that ESG issues can create both risks and opportunities that evolve over time \cite{dynamic_materiality}.

The integration of ESG considerations into investment decision-making is gaining momentum through initiatives like the Indonesia Stock Exchange's (IDX) Sustainable Finance Roadmap. The IDX has introduced ESG-based indices and is working toward mandatory ESG disclosures for listed companies, following the lead of global exchanges such as the NYSE and LSE \cite{idx_sustainable}. This development reflects the growing recognition that ESG factors are material to financial performance and risk management.

These global best practices are being adapted to the Indonesian context through consideration of local challenges such as deforestation, biodiversity conservation, and social inclusion. The result is a unique ESG landscape that maintains global alignment while addressing national priorities and development goals.

